  "Number": 14,
  "Question": "Define \\( a\\clubsuit b=a^2b-ab^2 .\\) Which of the following describes the set of points \\( (x, y) \\) for which \\( x\\clubsuit y=y\\clubsuit x ?\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "A finite set of points",
    "B": "One line",
    "C": "Two parallel lines",
    "D": "Two intersecting lines",
    "E": "Three lines"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "Apply the operation's definition in both operand orders:\n\\[\nx^2y-xy^2=y^2x-yx^2.\n\\]\nSince \\(y^2x=xy^2\\) and \\(yx^2=x^2y\\), collecting terms and factoring gives\n\\[\n2x^2y-2xy^2=0,\n\\qquad\n2xy(x-y)=0.\n\\]\nA product of real numbers is zero exactly when at least one factor is zero. Thus the condition holds exactly when\n\\[\nx=0,\\qquad y=0,\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad x=y.\n\\]\nThese describe the vertical axis, the horizontal axis, and the diagonal line \\(y=x\\). Every point on any of these lines satisfies the original equality, and the factorization leaves no other possibilities. They are three distinct lines, so the answer is choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{E}}\\).",
